AWS Glue Data Processing Pipeline Runbook
1. Overview
This runbook describes the operation, maintenance, and troubleshooting of the AWS Glue pipeline that processes hit-level web analytics data.

The pipeline:
• Reads raw hit-level data files from Amazon S3
• Validates schema and profiles metadata
• Performs business transformation to calculate revenue from search engines
• Writes processed output and logs back to S3

Primary goal:
Determine revenue generated from external search engines (Google, Yahoo, MSN/Bing) and identify the best-performing keywords.
2. Architecture Summary
Components:
• Amazon S3 – raw input data, processed output, logs, metadata
• AWS Glue – Spark-based ETL processing engine
• Python Glue Job – main pipeline logic
• Metadata Profiler – schema validation and profiling module

High Level Flow:
S3 Raw Data → AWS Glue Job → Metadata Profiling → Business Transformation → S3 Processed Output + Logs
3. S3 Folder Structure
analytics-hitdata-s3/
│
├── raw/
│   └── YYYY/MM/DD/HH-MM-SS/input_file.tab
│
├── processed/
│   └── YYYY/MM/DD/HH-MM-SS/output_file.tab
│
├── metadata/
│   └── metadata logs
│
├── logs/
│   └── pipeline logs and run summaries
│
└── checksums/
    └── checksum files used to detect duplicate processing
4. Glue Job Execution
Runtime Argument:
--input_key raw/YYYY/MM/DD/HH-MM-SS/input_file.tab

Example Run:
aws glue start-job-run --job-name search-keyword-revenue-pipeline --arguments '{"--input_key":"raw/2026/03/12/12-00-00/input_file.tab"}'
5. Pipeline Steps
Step 1 – Compute checksum
The pipeline calculates SHA256 checksum of the input file.

Step 2 – Skip unchanged files
If checksum exists in the checksums folder, the pipeline skips processing.

Step 3 – Read input file
Input file is read from S3 as tab-delimited text.

Step 4 – Metadata profiling
Schema validation and column statistics are computed.

Step 5 – Business transformation
Search engine referrers and keywords are extracted.
Purchases are detected using event_list.
Revenue is extracted from product_list.

Step 6 – Revenue attribution
Revenue is attributed to the most recent search engine interaction.

Step 7 – Aggregation
Revenue grouped by:
• Search Engine Domain
• Search Keyword

Step 8 – Write output
Results written to processed folder.

Step 9 – Write logs
Metadata logs, run summaries, and pipeline logs saved to S3.
6. Metadata Profiling
The MetadataProfiler validates:
• Missing columns
• Extra columns
• Column count mismatch

For each column it records:
• Min value
• Max value
• Null count
• Distinct count
7. Business Logic
Purchase Event Detection:
event_list contains event code '1'.

Revenue Extraction:
product_list format:
category;product;quantity;revenue

Search Engine Detection:
Based on referrer domain:
• google.*
• yahoo.*
• bing.* / msn.*
8. Output Format
Output File:
SearchKeywordPerformance.tab

Columns:
Search Engine Domain
Search Keyword
Revenue
9. Monitoring
Monitor via:
• AWS Glue job logs
• CloudWatch logs
• S3 pipeline logs

Key metrics:
• records_processed
• output_rows
• processing time
10. Troubleshooting
Issue: Job fails at startup
Check Glue worker configuration.

Issue: Missing columns
Verify metadata_config.json.

Issue: No revenue output
Check event_list and product_list parsing.

Issue: File skipped
Checksum already exists in checksums folder.
